    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**Yes \u2014 this is an exceptional, top-of-band offer for a Senior Product Manager (L6) at Google in the Bay Area.** \n\nMarket median compensation for an L6 PM at Google typically lands between **$450,000 and $530,000/year**. Your offer significantly exceeds those numbers across every component.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Year-by-Year Financial Breakdown\n\nGoogle uses a **front-loaded 4-year vesting schedule** for new-hire equity grants:\n* **Year 1:** 33% ($442,200)\n* **Year 2:** 33% ($442,200)\n* **Year 3:** 22% ($294,800)\n* **Year 4:** 12% ($160,800)\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 | $52,000 |\n| **New-Hire RSUs** | $442,200 | $442,200 | $294,800 | $160,800 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $200,000 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| **Estimated Refresher Vesting** | \u2014 | ~$30,000+ | ~$70,000+ | ~$120,000+ |\n| **Total Target Comp** | **~$954,200** | **~$784,200** | **~$676,800** | **~$592,800** |\n\n#### Comparison with Meta:\n* **Current Meta Comp:** ~$526,000/yr (with ~$200,000 vesting over the next 12 months).\n* **Google Year 1:** **~$954,200** (an increase of over $420,000).\n* **4-Year Annualized Average (excluding refreshers):** **~$697,000/yr** (a sustained ~32% bump over your current Meta package).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away:** **No.** Google is your top choice, the team and role excite you, and the offer is a massive step up financially.\n* **Accept vs. Negotiate:** \n  * Because you do not have competing offers, and this package is already pushed close to the ceiling of Google\u2019s L6 PM bands, an aggressive negotiation carries unnecessary friction.\n  * However, you can make a **gentle, one-time closing ask** tied directly to signing immediately. In Big Tech recruiting, a single reasonable counter-offer framed cooperatively will not cause an offer to be rescinded.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For (If You Negotiate)\n\nBecause your base is already near the top of the posted range ($260k out of $271k) and your sign-on is already very large ($200k), the easiest levers to tweak are rounding up the equity or nudging the base to the top of the band:\n\n1. **Option A (Equity Focus \u2014 Best Leverage):** Ask to increase the RSU grant from **$1.34M to $1.45M** over 4 years.\n2. **Option B (Base Focus):** Ask to adjust base salary from **$260,000 to $270,000** (the ceiling of the posted range).\n\nOffer to sign immediately if they can meet you on that point.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Negotiation Script\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this package together. I'm genuinely thrilled about the team, the scope of the role, and the opportunity at Google \u2014 it is my clear first choice.*\n>\n> *I took time to review the numbers against my current trajectory at Meta. While the overall offer is strong, if we could round the equity grant up to **$1.45M** (or alternatively, adjust base salary to **$270,000**), I am ready to sign the offer today.*\n>\n> *Is that something we can make happen?\"*\n\n### Bottom Line\nIf Google agrees, you capture extra upside. If they hold firm and say the current package is at the absolute cap for L6, **accept the offer enthusiastically** \u2014 you are already securing one of the strongest L6 PM compensation packages in the industry.",
